criteria:
Inclusion Criteria:

* Age between 18 and 90 years;
* Moderate-severe MDE as part of a unipolar or bipolar disorder (including psychotic and catatonic features) according to DSM-5-TR criteria (APA, 2022);
* Score ≥ 17 at the HDRS-17 at the inclusion interview (HAMILTON, 1960);
* Ability to understand the clinical investigation plan and comply with study visits;
* ECT indicated by the referring physician for MDE;
* Ability to tolerate the necessary adaptations to certain treatments (discontinuation, dosage, frequency of intake) for the proper conduct of ECT/FEAST (lithium, anticonvulsants with the exception of lamotrigine) and the restriction of benzodiazepine dosage to ≤ 3 mg/day of lorazepam equivalent (Boylan et al., 2000, Nahas et al., 2013, Sahlem et al., 2020, Youssef et al., 2021);
* Written informed consent.

Exclusion Criteria:

* Previous diagnosis of neurologic disease: neurodegenerative disease, epilepsy, encephalopathy, delirium tremens or memory disorder, …;
* Score \< 23 on the MoCA at inclusion interview (Nasreddine et al., 2005);
* Other neurological diseases affecting the central nervous system or head trauma in progress or having had a cognitive impact according to the anamnesis and the clinician's assessment. This include: space-occupying cerebral lesions, such as tumors or hematomas, cerebrovascular aneurysms or malformations, recent stroke (hemorrhagic or ischemic), other causes of increased intracranial pressure (e.g., hydrocephalus, meningitis, encephalitis), skull defects (which may include metal devices in or near the site of stimulation), metal devices, such as screws and plates or metal meshes, may preclude specific stimulation patterns but may not preclude treatment administration completely, implanted active stimulatory devices (e.g., Deep Brain Stimulation devices). Stimulation devices may pose a risk to the patient if the flow of current during treatment damages active components of the device (i.e. potential for catastrophic failure of the battery);
* General condition and/or comorbidities not allowing general anesthesia to be performed;
* Severe and unstable pulmonary conditions increasing the risks of problematic airway management and aspiration during and immediately after ECT according to the anesthesiologist's assessment such as chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), asthma, pneumonia;
* Severe and unstable cardiovascular conditions substantially increasing risks according to the anesthesiologist's assessment, such as recent and/or severe myocardial infarction, unstable angina, uncompensated congestive heart failure, vascular aneurysm or stenosis, clinically significant arrhythmias, uncontrolled hypertension or hypotension;
* Concomitant psychotic disorder (schizophrenia, schizoaffective disorder) according to DSM-5-TR or than MED with psychotic features (APA, 2022);
* Moderate to severe substance use disorder in the 3 months preceding the inclusion in the study (except caffeine and nicotine) (G. L. Sahlem et al., 2016, Youssef et al., 2021);
* Rapid-cycling bipolar disorder (Youssef et al., 2021);
* Having received an acute course of ECT in the 3 months preceding the inclusion in the study;
* Participation in another interventional research protocol other than magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) studies);
* Patient deprived of their liberty by judicial or administrative decision;
* Patient under legal protection;
* Pregnant and breastfeeding women;
* Patient not affiliated to a social security or other social protection scheme.
